KG42.register("rules-cd68265d9765cec5a296dc37",{"id":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding::v141-c-R012","paper":"25-p2p_ancillary_bidding","record":{"rule_id":"v141-c-R012","mechanism_id":"v141-a-M03-ancillary-service","name":"Consumer participation","process_stage":"participation","market_meaning":"Consumers may provide ancillary services for the power utility by increasing or decreasing their power consumption. Scope: Consumer participation in ancillary-service provision from demand-side P2P energy trading communities, with service time scales ranging from several minutes to several hours.","independence_rationale":"Relations governed by the approved function participation.consumer under the fixed relation-ownership template. Conditional branches retain their explicit scope in local constraints.","V":["participation.consumer"],"E":[],"omega":{"market_type":"DEM","network_level":null,"jurisdiction":null,"pricing_scope":null,"time_scale":null,"participant_types":[],"trading_stage":"participation","context_text":"Consumer participation in ancillary-service provision from demand-side P2P energy trading communities, with service time scales ranging from several minutes to several hours.","unknown_fields":["network_level","jurisdiction","pricing_scope","time_scale","participant_types"],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E03"]},"x":[],"theta":[],"phi":[{"constraint_id":"v141-c-R012-C001","kind":"procedural","role":"core","statement":"Consumers may provide ancillary services for the power utility by increasing or decreasing their power consumption. Scope: Consumer participation in ancillary-service provision from demand-side P2P energy trading communities, with service time scales ranging from several minutes to several hours.","expression":null,"formalization_origin":"not_formalized","template_id":null,"members":["participation.consumer"],"variable_refs":[],"parameter_refs":[],"evidence_ids":["v141-b-g01-E02","v141-b-g01-E03"]}],"pi":[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p02-c2r3__v141-a-M03-participation","page":2,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"in trading and ancillary service provision at the same time? Are they or better or worse off? t­ ed ","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-02.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"It is worth noting that ancillary service is a broad concept, which includes a wide range of services with various time scales (e.g. seconds, minutes, hours and even longer) and various purposes (e.g. frequency response, operating reserve, voltage support, constraint management, and peak shaving / valley filling). Ancillary services can be provided by the resources at both the supply and demand sides, and when at the demand side, ancillary services are usually provided through demand response. The ancillary services considered in this paper are those that can be provided through generation/demand increase/reduction from demand-side peer-to-peer energy trading communities, with the time scales ranging from several minutes to several hours. The ancillary services considered in this paper have some level of generality, because they cover a range of time scales and are not associated with specific purposes (e.g. they can be used for voltage support, operating reserve and peak shaving as long as the time scales fit). However, there are also some other ancillary services that have not been covered in this paper, such as frequency response with a second-by-second time scale, which are future research topics."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","location":{"location_id":"src-212077b9a1-p03-c1r1__v141-a-M03-participation","page":3,"printed_page":null,"section":null,"anchor_text":"Y. Zhou et al. onsite generation can fully satisfy the demand. Therefore, customers co with distribu","equation_id":null,"figure_id":null,"table_id":null,"image_ref":"pages/page-03.png","occurrence_role":"definition"},"source_text":"In the ancillary service mechanism, both prosumers and consumers can provide ancillary services for the power utility. For providing ancillary services, consumers can increase/decrease their power consumption, while prosumers can increase/decrease their power generation or consumption. In this paper, we use the general term ‘customers’, which can include both prosumers and consumers."}],"field_notes":{"empty_E_reason":"C explicitly supplied only source-backed unary facts for this function; other graph gaps remain reviewable.","empty_x_reason":"The source evidence grants consumers the option to provide ancillary services by increasing or decreasing power consumption but defines no optimized decision variable or design selection for this participation rule. No x is declared from v141-b-g01-E02 or v141-b-g01-E03.","empty_theta_reason":"The source evidence gives only the qualitative scope 'several minutes to several hours' and does not define a numerical time scale, coefficient, price, or other fixed parameter for this participation rule. No theta is declared from v141-b-g01-E02 or v141-b-g01-E03."},"evidence_basis":"explicit","implementation_status":"unimplemented","status":"needs_review","lead_ids":["v141-a-M03-participation"],"extras":{"decision_anchor":"participation.consumer","graph_fact_supports":[[{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E03","quote":"In the ancillary service mechanism, both prosumers and consumers can provide ancillary services for the power utility. For providing ancillary services, consumers can increase/decrease their power consumption, while prosumers can increase/decrease their power generation or consumption."},{"evidence_id":"v141-b-g01-E02","quote":"The ancillary services considered in this paper are those that can be provided through generation/demand increase/reduction from demand-side peer-to-peer energy trading communities, with the time scales ranging from several minutes to several hours."}]],"C2_formalization":{"graph_sha256":"20de351be28d2f072fdb85707b0106d8f8a9a7b5b806bc5480322593b1727653","applicability":"non_mathematical","reason":"The supplied evidence defines an eligibility/participation rule: consumers may provide ancillary services to the power utility by increasing or decreasing power consumption, within a qualitative time-scale scope. It states no equation, optimized decision variable, fixed parameter, or quantitative relation for this participation rule. The rule is therefore treated as procedural/non-mathematical rather than as a failed formalization.","bindings":[{"object_id":"v141-c-R012-C001","node_ids":["participation.consumer"],"edge_ids":[],"rationale":"The constraint is a unary participation/admission rule owned by the frozen node participation.consumer. The graph contains no edge for this rule, consistent with the source describing consumer eligibility and the direction of consumption adjustment without a relational formula. Evidence v141-b-g01-E03 states that consumers can provide ancillary services by increasing/decreasing power consumption, and v141-b-g01-E02 gives the ancillary-service scope and time-scale qualification."}],"revision_requests":[]}},"quarantined_references":[]}});
